  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you visit the same country often enough that a recurring monthly plan beats buying a fresh eSIM every time, or if you'd rather buy from an established MVNO than a smaller reseller. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you travel often to different places and want a data balance that rolls over between trips, backed by a real refund guarantee if your plans change. Both sit in the pricier bracket on this site, so neither is the pick if rock-bottom cost per gigabyte is your main concern.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Recurring Subscription vs. Rollover Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Ubigi sells data the way a phone carrier does: one-off fixed-GB plans, or recurring monthly and annual subscriptions that renew and bill automatically. Ubigi's own help center is explicit that a monthly plan can't be cancelled until you've held it for three months minimum, and an annual plan is paid upfront and can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term. Whatever data goes unused in a given period is gone either way; Ubigi doesn't roll it over.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesim takes a different approach with its "Pay & Fly" plans: you top up a dollar balance instead of buying a fixed block, priced from $1.10/GB, and unused balance carries over to your next trip rather than expiring. There's no multi-month lock-in to worry about, and Yesim backs it with a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans, a step further than most eSIM providers on this site, which only refund a plan that never activated in the first place.
  </p>

