  <h2>Is Ubigi or Yesim the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country on a regular schedule and a recurring monthly plan beats buying fresh each time</li>
        <li>You already drive a connected car from one of Ubigi's automaker partners (BMW, Toyota, Jeep, and others run on the same Transatel network)</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO, even at a higher price, than a smaller reseller</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You travel frequently to different places and want a balance that rolls over instead of a plan tied to one trip</li>
        <li>You want the option of a real refund if you buy ahead and end up not needing the plan</li>
        <li>You'd rather not commit to Ubigi's 3-month minimum on a monthly plan or its non-refundable annual term</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ubigi's recurring plans are a real commitment, not just a label.</strong> A monthly subscription locks you in for three months before you can cancel it, and an annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term. Yesim's Pay &amp; Fly balance has no equivalent lock-in; you top up and spend down at your own pace.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither provider is the cheap option here.</strong> Ubigi runs roughly double what <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a> charge per gigabyte, and Yesim's day-based plans run from roughly $1.56 to $31 depending on destination, also well above what those two charge. If price per GB is what matters most, check Airhub or Holafly before buying either.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Ubigi vs Yesim FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Ubigi or Yesim</h3>
      <p>Neither is cheap compared to most providers on this site. On a straight per-GB basis Ubigi tends to come out ahead for data-heavy use; Yesim's day-based plans can work out cheaper for a short light-use trip, and its rollover Pay &amp; Fly balance avoids wasting unused data between trips.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either data allowance roll over between trips</h3>
      <p>Yesim's Pay &amp; Fly balance does: unused balance carries over to your next top-up instead of expiring. Ubigi's data does not roll over on any plan type, one-off, monthly, or annual.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I cancel or get a refund if I change my mind</h3>
      <p>Yesim advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans. Ubigi's one-off plans follow its standard policy, but a recurring monthly plan can't be cancelled until three months in, and an annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Who actually runs each of these</h3>
      <p>Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler now part of NTT Group, which also supplies the connectivity behind connected-car systems for automakers including BMW, Toyota, and Jeep. Yesim markets itself on automatic network switching across a claimed 800+ partner networks, a claim we haven't independently verified.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

